\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every epsilon in (0,1), is there N0 such that for every integer $N>=N0$ one can choose N real coefficients, each +1 or -1, so that the modulus of their polynomial is between (1-epsilon)sqrt(N) and (1+epsilon)sqrt(N) at every point of the unit circle?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every epsilon in (0,1), is there N0 such that for every integer $N>=N0$ one can choose N real coefficients, each +1 or -1, so that the modulus of their polynomial is between (1-epsilon)sqrt(N) and (1+epsilon)sqrt(N) at every point of the unit circle?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
